{\rtf1\ansi\deff0{\fonttbl{\f0 \fswiss Helvetica;}{\f1 \fmodern Courier;}}
{\colortbl;\red255\green0\blue0;\red0\green0\blue255;}
\widowctrl\hyphauto

{\pard \ql \f0 \fs24 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Detects clicks (tempo taps) in the audio input, works out the BPM and shows the delay time for the chosen subdivision. Map the Delay Time readout slider to the Replika XT delay parameter.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \fs24 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 PARAMETERS Delay Time [Readout] Output: delay time in ms (subdivision of detected tempo + offset). Map this. Detected BPM [Readout] Output: detected (quantized) tempo. Subdivision Note value the delay time is calculated for (1/4 = one beat). Threshold Input level (dB) a click must exceed to be detected. Min / Max BPM Accepted tempo range. Clicks outside it are ignored; double/triple/1.5x intervals are folded into the range (subclick / shuffle handling). Audio Input Channel Which channel(s) are analysed: stereo sum, left or right. Consistency Tolerance How much (%) consecutive click intervals may differ to still count as the same tempo. Larger = more forgiving of sloppy timing; a bigger difference is treated as a new tempo and the detection restarts. Lock Intervals How many consistent intervals are averaged before the readout updates. 2 = fastest lock (3 clicks); more = steadier, but slower. Output Hysteresis Readout changes only if the new tempo differs by more than this (%). Filters tiny jitter; 0 = follow every update. BPM Quantize Rounds the detected BPM to this step BEFORE anything else (so the ms value is derived from the rounded BPM). Off = no rounding. Delay Offset (ms) Added to the final delay time. Negative = shorter. Use it to shift the echo so it does not smear the next transient. Delay Offset (%) Scales the delay time (e.g. -1 = 1% shorter), so it follows tempo and subdivision. Applied before the ms offset, which is then added.\par}
}

{\rtf1\ansi\deff0{\fonttbl{\f0 \fswiss Helvetica;}{\f1 \fmodern Courier;}}
{\colortbl;\red255\green0\blue0;\red0\green0\blue255;}
\widowctrl\hyphauto

{\pard \ql \f0 \fs24 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Measures the tempo of incoming MIDI Clock (0xF8, 24 ticks per quarter note) with sample-accurate timing, works out the BPM and shows the delay time for the chosen subdivision. Map the Delay Time readout slider to a delay parameter (e.g. Replika XT). MIDI passes through unchanged.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \fs24 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 PARAMETERS Delay Time [Readout] Output: delay time in ms (subdivision of detected tempo + offsets). Map this. Detected BPM [Readout] Output: detected (quantized) tempo. Subdivision Note value the delay time is calculated for (1/4 = one beat). Min / Max BPM Accepted tempo range. Beats outside it are ignored. Consistency Tolerance How much (%) consecutive beat lengths may differ to still count as the same tempo. Larger = more forgiving; a bigger difference is treated as a new tempo and the detection restarts. Lock Intervals How many consistent beats are averaged before the readout updates. 1 = fastest lock; more = steadier, but slower. Output Hysteresis Readout changes only if the new tempo differs by more than this (%). Filters tiny jitter; 0 = follow every update. BPM Quantize Rounds the detected BPM to this step BEFORE anything else (so the ms value is derived from the rounded BPM). Off = no rounding. Delay Offset (ms) Added to the final delay time. Negative = shorter. Use it to shift the echo so it does not smear the next transient. Delay Offset (%) Scales the delay time (e.g. -1 = 1% shorter), so it follows tempo and subdivision. Applied before the ms offset, which is then added.\par}
}

{\rtf1\ansi\deff0{\fonttbl{\f0 \fswiss Helvetica;}{\f1 \fmodern Courier;}}
{\colortbl;\red255\green0\blue0;\red0\green0\blue255;}
\widowctrl\hyphauto

{\pard \ql \f0 \fs24 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Inverts the values of drawbar controllers (CC 16-24, value -> 127 - value), because the Nord Stage 3 drawbars work in the opposite direction to the drawbars in NI B3 / Kontakt Organs. All other MIDI messages pass through unchanged.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \fs24 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 PARAMETERS MIDI Channel Channel on which the drawbar CCs are inverted (All = every channel).\par}
}

{\rtf1\ansi\deff0{\fonttbl{\f0 \fswiss Helvetica;}{\f1 \fmodern Courier;}}
{\colortbl;\red255\green0\blue0;\red0\green0\blue255;}
\widowctrl\hyphauto

{\pard \ql \f0 \fs24 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Copies or re-routes MIDI Control Change messages from chosen source channels to chosen destination channels, so e.g. one volume / expression fader can drive several instruments (strings on channels 2-4) at once. All other MIDI is untouched. Only the selected channels get messages, so the MIDI bus is not flooded with 16 channels.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \fs24 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 PARAMETER Original CC Keep original = the incoming CC passes through and copies are added. Remove original = a matching CC is NOT passed on, only sent to the destination channels (e.g. CC arrives on ch 1, goes only to ch 5, 6, 7).\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \fs24 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 GRIDS (click to toggle, click and drag to paint many at once) Source channels Channels the CC is read from (several can be selected). Destination channels Channels that receive the CC. With Keep original, a copy is never sent back to the channel the message came from (it would be a duplicate). CC numbers Which controllers are copied (0-127). Hover a cell to see the CC name, e.g. "CC 7: Channel Volume (MSB)". Buttons: "0-119" selects all real controllers (not the channel-mode messages 120-127, e.g. All Notes Off), "None" clears the grid. Search Click the search field (a blinking cursor shows it is active), then type to find CCs by name or number (e.g. "vol", "pan", "64"); matches get a yellow outline. Backspace deletes, Esc clears. "Add found" / "Remove found" switch all matches on / off in one click. The grid state is stored in hidden parameters, so it is saved with presets and projects.\par}
}

